      answer: 'Neither model is "right" — they answer different questions. Last-touch answers: "which channel was present at conversion?" It over-credits closers (SEM, retargeting). Multi-touch linear answers: "which channels were present at any point in the journey?" It spreads credit but treats all touches as equally influential regardless of recency or position. Recommendation: use a model matched to the decision being made. For budget allocation across awareness vs conversion channels, use data-driven attribution (Shapley values) if enough conversion volume exists (above 10k conversions per month). For near-term CAC optimization (which keywords or creatives to bid more on), last-touch is still the right signal. Communication strategy: never present the switch as "the old model was wrong" — it will invalidate 2 years of decisions and create defensive reactions. Instead: "We now have a multi-touch view that complements our existing last-touch view. Here is what each tells us and when to use each." Show both models side-by-side on a reconciliation slide. The 38% to 12% podcast shift deserves investigation: are podcast ads genuinely less effective, or are they upper-funnel brand drivers that appear elsewhere in the journey as a precursor to conversion? That question requires an incrementality test, not just a model switch.',
